{"version":"2026-10-03d","about":"Corridor line geometry for rail.html. Rail lines follow Natural Earth 1:10m railroads (public domain) between cited stations (shortest path on the rail graph, Douglas-Peucker simplified: hi = 1.5 km, lo = 4 km, 3 decimals). Generalised at 1:10M, so geometric length runs 2-5% shorter than timetable km; on-screen distances always use the cited sources, never the line length. approx = lines opened after the Natural Earth snapshot or missing from it, drawn station to station (the Caucasus link Tbilisi–Marabda–Tsalka–Akhalkalaki–Kartsakhi–Kars follows the Baku–Tbilisi–Kars stations; gauge change 1,520/1,435 mm at Akhalkalaki). sea = maritime links, not rail. Sea links show a connection, not a sailing track (Busan-Vladivostok follows the East Sea coast; Aktau-Alat rounds the Absheron peninsula). routes = the full line chain each corridor actually runs on (its own line, then the shared TSR trunk from the junction, then Moscow-Brest); the screen highlights the whole chain when a corridor is chosen. countries = faint base-layer country names with candidate anchors and a minimum zoom.","checked":"2026-10-03","sources":{"G1":{"org":"Natural Earth","title":"1:10m Cultural Vectors, Railroads","url":"https://www.naturalearthdata.com/downloads/10m-cultural-vectors/railroads/","terms":"public-domain"},"G2":{"org":"Wikipedia","title":"GeoData article coordinates","url":"https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Extension:GeoData","terms":"cc-by-sa"},"G3":{"org":"OpenStreetMap","title":"Station nodes via Nominatim","url":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","terms":"odbl"}},"lines":[{"id":"TSR","corr":"TSR","role":"trunk","kind":"rail","hi":[[43.111,131.879],[43.196,131.946],[43.246,132.046],[43.3,132.073],[43.514,131.892],[43.554,131.907],[43.603,131.984],[43.835,131.952],[43.904,132.026],[43.963,132.031],[44.007,132.081],[44.008,132.135],[44.028,132.182],[44.039,132.259],[44.106,132.354],[44.348,132.542],[44.477,132.572],[44.526,132.65],[44.564,132.769],